1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,200
 Hey everybody, Ryan here and welcome back to our oral surgery series.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,000
 This video will be about implants and implant surgery.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,360
 And this is a vast topic that could involve an enormous series just by itself.

4
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:24,000
 So this will just be a cursory look at implants and implant surgery, focusing again on the

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,900
 high yield topics for the board exam, and also for your own edification.

6
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:36,200
 So why do we place implants, of course, to replace a missing tooth?

7
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:42,600
 Here is a really nice schematic showing an implant being placed into bone, and here is

8
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:49,720
 a radiograph of the finished result with a restored implant crown.

9
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:55,120
 Here are indications for placing an implant, we have a list here, uncontrolled diabetes

10
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:57,940
 and immunocompromised patients.

11
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:01,600
 These are both at higher risk of infection.

12
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:07,400
 The volume and height of bone needs to be sufficient in order to support an implant.

13
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:13,780
 Bizphosphinate therapy, that patient will be at risk of Bizphosphinate related osteonacrosis

14
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:16,000
 of the jaws or branch.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,200
 And you may be noticing that a lot of these are contraindications for tooth extraction

16
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:27,240
 as well, and there's quite a bit of overlap between these two lists.

17
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:33,840
 Bruxism, a patient who is constantly clenching and grinding their teeth, may not be the best

18
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,360
 fit for an implant.

19
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:41,520
 Smoking is a relative contraindication, again all of these are relative contraindications.

20
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:48,440
 A history of head and neck radiation, another familiar site from the tooth extraction, contraindication

21
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:53,680
 list, a patient with cleft palate, and adolescents.

22
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:58,440
 Adolescents are not done growing, so if we place an implant in one part of the bone,

23
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:02,520
 as they continue to grow and their jaws continue to grow, that implant may not be in the place

24
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:04,680
 that we want it to be.

25
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:10,520
 So we need to wait until 2022-24, until that bone is completely done growing and we don't

26
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,280
 have to worry about that implant moving around.

27
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:22,220
 Now along with age, old age is not a contraindication for placing an implant.

28
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:26,480
 Just because you're older doesn't mean you can't have an implant and can't have a really

29
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:29,120
 nice result with an implant.

30
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:35,400
 So you may get a board's question that poses the scenario where you have an older patient,

31
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:39,160
 and that alone is not a contraindication for receiving an implant.

32
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:44,840
 All of these are relative contraindications.

33
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:49,000
 So we have three main categories of implants.

34
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,320
 These are big picture categories.

35
00:02:52,320 --> 00:03:00,040
 Sub-periosteal, and if we unpack this name, that means under the periosteum, and it literally

36
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:04,720
 rests under the periosteum layer.

37
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:10,400
 And so it's not screwed into the bone at all, it's laying underneath the periosteum just

38
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,320
 like a blanket.

39
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:18,680
 And this metal framework has extensions that poke through the oral mucosa shown in pink

40
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:23,960
 here for retention of a prosthesis.

41
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:29,080
 So there's no true osteointegration with this implant, and we'll talk about osteointegration

42
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:35,680
 later in the video, and therefore has a very poor prognosis.

43
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:41,840
 So it's rare to see these around at all today, and that's because most of them, if they were

44
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:49,920
 placed a while ago, have since failed, and they're not a great option for implant therapy.

45
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:53,520
 Next we have trans-austeal, or trans-austeous.

46
00:03:53,520 --> 00:04:01,440
 Either way you write it, that can be, that can refer to trans-meaning through and osteo-meaning

47
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:02,440
 the bone.

48
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,800
 So these implants are placed through the bone.

49
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:13,280
 Now these are exclusive to the mandibular anterior region because it requires an extra-oral approach.

50
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:20,440
 You drill this one through the inferior border of the mandible and into the oral mucosa.

51
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:28,440
 Usually they involve two to four trans-mandibular implants in order to support a larger framework.

52
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:36,280
 Again, these are very rare, it's a very involved surgery, and do not have great success either.

53
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:40,920
 So that leaves us with the third category, endosteal or endosseous.

54
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:45,720
 We unpack this word, it means "into" or "in" the bone.

55
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:49,280
 And so the endosteal implants are placed into the bone.

56
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:56,320
 Now these are available in many different forms, there's a blade shape, a rainness frame,

57
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:00,960
 and the ones that are depicted here that we see by far most often.

58
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:06,000
 And what we'll talk about throughout this video is the root form implant.

59
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,200
 It's generally cylindrical, and it's called a root form implant because it's shaped like

60
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,040
 a tooth root.

61
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:17,480
 And so let's talk about the endosteal implant.

62
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:23,680
 So an endosteal implant has four primary components, if we boil it down.

63
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:31,320
 And from bottom to top in the schematic, we have the implant body, the abutment, the abutment

64
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,840
 screw, and the implant crown.

65
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:40,640
 So now in the next couple slides, let's unpack each of these components.

66
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:47,680
 So the implant body, also called just the implant or the implant fixture, is shown here in

67
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,240
 the top right.

68
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:55,320
 It's usually axis symmetric, which means if I were to draw a long axis down the center

69
00:05:55,320 --> 00:06:01,600
 of this implant, it would be symmetric around that long axis.

70
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:10,040
 The basic procedure for placing an implant such as this one is to drill a hole into the

71
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:11,040
 bone.

72
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:17,720
 And we sequentially enlarge that osteotomy, which is a fancy name for the bone hole.

73
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:22,760
 So we start with a smaller drill, much smaller than the diameter of this implant, then we

74
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:28,200
 go to a larger one, a larger one, and finally one that's large enough to create a hole that

75
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:33,520
 will support this implant being drilled into it.

76
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:38,860
 So the reason why we sequentially enlarge the osteotomy is to, number one, reduce the

77
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:41,120
 amount of heat generated.

78
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:47,160
 And this helps to keep the bone cells alive, which we desperately want, and having a sharp

79
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:53,880
 drill, and also using copious irrigation, helps with reducing heat generated as well.

80
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:59,800
 Secondly, it helps to maintain the axis with freehand surgery.

81
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,560
 So what the heck does that mean?

82
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:09,240
 Well when we're doing an implant placement, we can be using a stent or a template of sorts,

83
00:07:09,240 --> 00:07:14,880
 and we'll talk about that a little bit later, or you can be placing the implant freehanded.

84
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:21,400
 Now of course you have radiographs, or better yet, a cone beam CT image, basically a three

85
00:07:21,400 --> 00:07:27,160
 dimensional x-ray, so you know how wide the bone is, how deep the bone is, and you know

86
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:35,080
 what angulation a place that implant at, but being able to check the hole at each step,

87
00:07:35,080 --> 00:07:38,520
 you can check how you're doing with your angulation.

88
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:44,520
 So you start with a really small hole, you put in a little indicator, and you can say,

89
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:50,720
 well, my hole's a little bit too angled distally, so next time when I go up to the next bigger

90
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:53,960
 drill, I can correct that angulation.

91
00:07:53,960 --> 00:08:01,520
 So it gives you some opportunities to correct and maintain a proper axis.

92
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:08,640
 The implant body is usually inserted into tapped holes, and a tapped hole in carpentry,

93
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:14,600
 or any trade for that matter, is a hole drilled prior to a screw being inserted.

94
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:20,920
 It's similar to a pilot hole, except that it has the threads cut into the inside surface

95
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:22,360
 of the hole.

96
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:31,480
 However, some of these implants are self-tapping, and you can see that by these little gouges

97
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:38,080
 that are made in the apical part of the implant, and so they essentially cut and create their

98
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:45,200
 own threads as they're drilled into the bone hole, and thus do not require this step.

99
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:50,280
 So that's some big picture information about the implant body.

100
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:52,680
 Now let's talk about the abutment.

101
00:08:52,680 --> 00:09:01,000
 So the abutment is the part of the implant schematic here that is placed onto you and

102
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,760
 into the implant body.

103
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:09,440
 So the abutment can be broken down into two big categories, one piece abutments and two

104
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:10,440
 piece abutments.

105
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:17,640
 So let's spend some time digging into the differences between these two categories.

106
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:25,880
 So the one piece abutment combines the abutment and the abutment screw into one component.

107
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:30,800
 So if we go back to the schematic, that's combining the abutment and the abutment screw

108
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:33,320
 into one big piece.

109
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:41,360
 So the screw is actually a part of the abutment, and so the whole thing screws into the inside

110
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:49,560
 of the implant body, and this beautiful cross section image here shows that abutment adapting

111
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:54,800
 to the inside of the implant body, and then that screw holding it in place.

112
00:09:54,800 --> 00:10:02,360
 So here is the implant body, the abutment screw, which is part of the larger abutment.

113
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:09,440
 It has no anti-rotation component, which we'll talk about in a later slide.

114
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:17,480
 Notice it has a smooth conical shape, which is called a conus, C-O-N-U-S, that wedges into

115
00:10:17,480 --> 00:10:24,840
 the top of the implant to ensure proper seating.

116
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:31,880
 Now more commonly used is the two piece abutment, and so the abutment is held to the implant

117
00:10:31,880 --> 00:10:38,920
 body via a screw that goes down the center of both.

118
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:45,080
 So in the two piece abutment, the abutment screw and the abutment are separate components.

119
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:52,240
 And so in the cross section image here, you can see the outside of the abutment, which

120
00:10:52,240 --> 00:11:01,000
 is here and here, interfaces with the implant body, which is this part here, and this part

121
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:11,080
 here, and the screw, the abutment screw, that drills down the center of both the abutment

122
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,040
 and the implant body.

123
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:21,000
 And this is a bit difficult to comprehend, but if we break it down into the four major

124
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:26,200
 components of an implant, hopefully that can make it a little bit easier.

125
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:33,920
 So this abutment does have an anti-rotation component, because you can seat it and lock

126
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:40,200
 it in place, and then place the abutment screw through that whole assembly.

127
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:45,320
 And again, we'll talk more about the anti-rotation component a little bit later, and why that

128
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:53,080
 makes the two piece abutment a little bit more desirable than the one piece in most cases.

129
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:57,040
 And finally, let's talk about the implant crown.

130
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:01,800
 So the implant crown itself can be broken down into two categories, the screw-retained

131
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:06,080
 crown, and a cement-retained crown.

132
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:13,600
 So for the screw-retained crown, we would place a screw through the crown into the implant,

133
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:20,840
 and in general, the abutment and crown of this type are fabricated as one component.

134
00:12:20,840 --> 00:12:26,720
 And then the abutment screw is placed through the entire piece into the implant.

135
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:33,840
 So this is technically a, now we're combining the crown and the abutment components, and

136
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:38,360
 then the abutment screw shown in gold here is drilled through both of them.

137
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:43,160
 So this is technically would be an example of a two piece abutment, where the screw and

138
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:45,480
 abutment are separate.

139
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:51,720
 Now the cons of this type are that we have a screw access hole, and so the screw access

140
00:12:51,720 --> 00:12:57,520
 hole is typically placed in the central fossa, in the posterior teeth, or in the singular

141
00:12:57,520 --> 00:13:00,800
 area of the anterior teeth.

142
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:09,280
 Now the screw and the access hole is very apparent, and could create an aesthetic problem.

143
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:16,640
 And what we typically would do is place some Teflon tape into the hole, and then place

144
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:20,200
 a composite plug to fill in the rest of the hole.

145
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:26,280
 So you need to position the access hole in a non-aesthetic area to avoid, even though

146
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:31,720
 you can match the color somewhat with composite, you usually want to leave it a little bit,

147
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:37,600
 a little bit distinguishable from the implant crown in case you had to go back and access

148
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:40,200
 that screw at a later time.

149
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:47,960
 Obviously, this screw access hole can't be coming through the facial surface of an incisor.

150
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:53,560
 So this is why the implant has to be planned thoroughly ahead of time to ensure proper

151
00:13:53,560 --> 00:14:00,320
 placement and angulation of the implant, so that the crown and this screw access hole

152
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:06,720
 can be angled in a proper direction.

153
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:11,880
 So now the pros of this screw retain crown, one of them I already mentioned, is that it

154
00:14:11,880 --> 00:14:14,160
 can be removed at a later time.

155
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:18,960
 All you have to do is take out the composite plug, take out the Teflon tape, and then you

156
00:14:18,960 --> 00:14:24,640
 can take the screw out, remove the screw, and take the crown off, clean the components,

157
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:28,360
 and do other things that are beneficial.

158
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:33,200
 You have no cement to worry about with this screw retain crown, which is a benefit.

159
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:36,240
 We'll talk more about that in just a little bit.

160
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:42,740
 And the big one is that it takes up less vertical restorative space than the cement retained,

161
00:14:42,740 --> 00:14:49,040
 so it's better for restricted restorative space areas.

162
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:56,240
 Now the cement retained crown is where the abutment is attached as a separate component

163
00:14:56,240 --> 00:15:03,000
 from the crown, so these two are now separate, and you can have either the one piece abutment

164
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:09,560
 with its own screw, or the two piece abutment with a separate screw.

165
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:14,680
 So like I say here, the abutment is attached separately to the implant.

166
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:19,920
 The pros for this one are that there's no screw access hole, so it's typically better

167
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:26,680
 for anterior teeth and incisors, because now we don't have to worry about having a screw

168
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:33,000
 access hole in an aesthetic area that could compromise our final result.

169
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,520
 The big con is, well I guess there are two of them.

170
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:42,760
 Once it's cemented, it can't be removed unless it's physically cut off, and the one I have

171
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:50,400
 listed here, which is probably most frequently tested, is that cement may be trapped subgingivally.

172
00:15:50,400 --> 00:15:56,240
 And so if it's trapped under the gingiva, it can cause peri implantitis.

173
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:02,080
 To remember that's essentially peridontal disease around an implant instead of around

174
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:03,080
 a tooth.

175
00:16:03,080 --> 00:16:04,080
 So you have the same symptoms.

176
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:10,000
 You have gingiva inflammation and bone loss, and this is a big, big problem.

177
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:15,400
 And cement, a lot of research shows that cement can be a big cause of this.

178
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:22,100
 So that's a big negative, and typically why it's considered standard of care after placing

179
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:28,480
 a any implant, but particularly for cement retained implants, to take a periapical radiograph

180
00:16:28,480 --> 00:16:34,120
 or a bite wing and so you can see if there's cement that was left over and not cleaned

181
00:16:34,120 --> 00:16:36,080
 out after placement.

182
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:41,080
 All right, so those were the four components of implants.

183
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:46,160
 Now let's talk about one piece versus two piece implants.

184
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:51,680
 So the one piece implant, this is not to be confused with the one and two piece abutments,

185
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:59,080
 the one piece implant is where the implant body and the abutment are physically attached

186
00:16:59,080 --> 00:17:00,400
 together.

187
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:05,520
 And so this whole component, now we don't even have an abutment screw, we just have the

188
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:13,840
 abutment and the implant combined together and they're drilled into bone as one unit.

189
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:20,960
 One way to, one helpful way to view the abutment component is as the quote unquote crown prep

190
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:24,160
 of this metal tooth.

191
00:17:24,160 --> 00:17:32,440
 And it's like the metal crown prep that the implant crown will sit onto.

192
00:17:32,440 --> 00:17:37,200
 And the problem with a one piece implant is that you cannot correct the angle between

193
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:43,280
 the two components because they're attached as one piece, whereas once an implant's placed

194
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:50,520
 and maybe you want to correct for an angulation error, you can purchase an abutment that has

195
00:17:50,520 --> 00:17:58,520
 a certain angle built into it and so you can correct that post implant surgery.

196
00:17:58,520 --> 00:18:06,320
 The two piece implant is much more common and the implant and the final abutment are

197
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:08,840
 a separate components.

198
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:15,600
 And so the final abutment and the crown are typically placed at the same appointment.

199
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:21,180
 Probably several weeks after the implant has been placed to allow the bone to heal and

200
00:18:21,180 --> 00:18:25,040
 osteointegrate, which again we'll talk about a little bit later.

201
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:30,760
 And so once the abutment is in place, the crown can either be cemented on top, which

202
00:18:30,760 --> 00:18:36,720
 is the cement retained or the crown, which includes the abutment are screwed together

203
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:40,520
 into the implant, which is the screw retained crown.

204
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:47,200
 All right, let's talk about that anti-rotation component that I mentioned before.

205
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:51,920
 And so the anti-rotation component can take several different forms.

206
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:58,720
 Here we see an internal hex, which is this is the top of the implant body and it has

207
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:04,760
 this index that's built into the inside of the implant so that when you place an abutment,

208
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:11,560
 it can only go in a certain amount of ways, it locks in like a lock and key so that once

209
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:18,080
 the abutment is fully seated, it can't spin around and rotate and rock back and forth.

210
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:26,420
 Here's an external hex, which shows a similar index, but this time this hexagon is going

211
00:19:26,420 --> 00:19:32,440
 to lock into the abutment in this direction, this way the abutment is going to lock down

212
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,280
 into the implant body.

213
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:42,240
 So both of these are providing the same objective to prevent rotation of the abutment and to

214
00:19:42,240 --> 00:19:45,480
 provide stabilization of the abutment.

215
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:53,560
 So essentially it's preventing spinning and preventing rocking back and forth of the abutment

216
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:58,320
 and spinning and rocking are very undesirable outcomes.

217
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:05,400
